Kevin Wayne <killedbyafoo@yahoo.com> writes:
> [ Shopkeepers stay mad across multiple games if you keep the same name. ]
> Gender, alignment, even species can change, but not the name. So the
> shopkeeper recognizes you by name so that you can't, for example,
> poly yourself back into his good graces.

Come to think of it, why should this be? Unless the shopkeeper actually
witnesses the poly, how does he know that the new form is you? (For that
matter, how does he know your name to begin with?)

> After all, how much *worse* would it be on a multi-user system if you
> happened to choose the same name as someone else who had left a bones
> file with a hostile shopkeeper? "What the heck is he mad at *me* for?"

Happens to me at Best Buy all the time. Why should nethack be more fair
than RL?

Anyway, on a single-player system, a good defense is to not anger
shopkeepers, or to not leave alive those you do anger. If it bothers
you, throw together some wrapper script like this:

#!/usr/bin/perl -w
for($i=0; $i<12; $i++) { $name.=chr(int(rand(26))+ord('a')); }
exec "nethack", "-u", $name;
die("unable to exec nethack: $!\n");

Replace with your favorite Markov chain or reverse parse tree toy for
pronounceable names, or hit your favorite online name list or generator
for the mad flavah.


